# Source: forum t/222776 n=3890 (Oever González, not hosted)
# Upstream: n/a (forum post; evaluate against openwrt/mt76 master for upstreaming)
# Reason: fix both halves of the NTB NPU RX descriptor ownership handshake:
#         bound the consume chain walk to armed slots (dcache_inval_poc panic)
#         and arm refill slots only after the buffer address is visible
#         (zero-address DMA race / ring-drift deadlock)
From: Oever González <software@notengobattery.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 11:03:49 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: npu: fix the rx descriptor ownership handshake

The host and the NPU trade ownership of RX ring slots through the DONE
bit in the descriptor ctrl word: the NPU sets DONE once it has written a
frame, and treats a descriptor whose ctrl reads DONE-clear as a slot it
may write into. The driver gets both halves of that handshake wrong.

On consume, mt76_npu_dequeue() reads the chain length from the ring-tail
descriptor and walks it without ever comparing it against the number of
armed slots. PKT_COUNT is three bits, so the count itself is at most
seven, but nothing ties it to q->queued: whenever fewer slots are armed
than the count claims, the walk strides past them into entries holding
no valid buffer. DONE stays set on a consumed descriptor until refill
re-arms it, so the walk passes the DONE check and either re-delivers
already consumed pages or hands a garbage address to
dma_sync_single_for_cpu():

  Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address ffffffff80000700
  CPU: 2 PID: 1472 Comm: napi/phy0-77 Tainted: G O 6.12.94
  pc : dcache_inval_poc+0x28/0x58
  Call trace:
   dcache_inval_poc+0x28/0x58
   __dma_sync_single_for_cpu+0xc0/0xe0
   mt76_npu_rx_queue_init+0x2ec/0x4c0 [mt76]  (static mt76_npu_dequeue)
   __napi_poll+0x34/0x180
  Kernel panic - not syncing: Oops: Fatal exception in interrupt

This is a missing bound, not a decoding problem: the PKT_COUNT field
definition was corrected well before the trace above was taken, and the
walk is unbounded against the armed count regardless of how any
descriptor field is laid out.

The mid-chain bail-out is unsound for the same reason: it frees the
partial skb while the unconsumed ring slots still reference its pages,
and the refill in the same NAPI pass can re-arm those pages at the head,
leaving one page with two owners.

On refill, mt76_npu_fill_rx_queue() clears the descriptor before storing
the new buffer address:

	memset(&desc[q->head], 0, sizeof(*desc));
	desc[q->head].addr = e->dma_addr[0];

so the memset arms the slot while addr still reads zero, and nothing
orders the two stores. If the NPU takes the slot in that window it DMAs
a frame to address zero and advances CPU_IDX, which it owns, while the
host still expects its own refill there. The two sides' ring positions
drift by one per hit. The host's only index write is DMA_IDX, its
consume position, advanced on a successful dequeue, and that is exactly
the operation the drift breaks: the host waits for DONE on a slot the
NPU has already passed. The queue deadlocks with the driver reporting
every slot armed and the NPU reporting HOSTADPT_API_Q_FULL indefinitely,
and only re-probing the device recovers it.

Fix both halves:

 - never walk more slots than are armed, and verify the whole chain is
   DONE before touching any page, so a bad count becomes a parse error
   instead of memory corruption;
 - order the descriptor reads after the DONE checks with dma_rmb();
 - store ctrl last on refill, behind a dma_wmb(), so the buffer address
   is visible to the NPU before the slot is armed.

A wrong count within the armed region can still mis-frame packets, since
the PKT_COUNT layout is firmware-ABI dependent, but it can no longer
walk out of the ring.

Reproduced on a Gemtek W1700K (Airoha AN7581 + MT7996) by stopping the
flow-offload daemon while about 1.1 Gbit/s was in flight to a 6 GHz
client: 3 of 3 runs wedged before, with the RX queue head and tail
frozen at 511 armed slots and Q_FULL growing by exactly the delivery
shortfall; 5 of 5 survived after, at roughly 40k pps.

That reproduction ran on a revision of the dequeue fix that also cleared
ctrl on consume, which widens the refill window from the memset to the
whole span between consume and refill. The form here does not clear ctrl
on consume, so the window is narrower; the ordering defect and the
deadlock it produces are unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Oever González <software@notengobattery.com>
---
 npu.c | 30 +++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/npu.c b/npu.c
index dd954df..9746562 100644
--- a/npu.c
+++ b/npu.c
@@ -32,8 +32,16 @@ int mt76_npu_fill_rx_queue(struct mt76_dev *dev, struct mt76_queue *q)
 		page = virt_to_head_page(e->buf);
 		e->dma_addr[0] = page_pool_get_dma_addr(page) + offset;
 
-		memset(&desc[q->head], 0, sizeof(*desc));
+		desc[q->head].info = 0;
+		desc[q->head].data = 0;
 		desc[q->head].addr = e->dma_addr[0];
+		desc[q->head].rsv = 0;
+		/* the NPU takes a descriptor whose ctrl reads DONE-clear as a
+		 * slot it may write into, so arm it only once the new buffer
+		 * address is visible
+		 */
+		dma_wmb();
+		WRITE_ONCE(desc[q->head].ctrl, 0);
 
 		q->head = (q->head + 1) % q->ndesc;
 		q->queued++;
@@ -67,19 +75,27 @@ static struct sk_buff *mt76_npu_dequeue(struct mt76_dev *dev,
 	int i, nframes, index = q->tail;
 	struct sk_buff *skb = NULL;
 
+	if (!q->queued)
+		return NULL;
+
 	nframes = FIELD_GET(NPU_RX_DMA_PKT_COUNT_MASK, desc[index].info);
-	nframes = max_t(int, nframes, 1);
+	/* a chain can never span more slots than are armed */
+	nframes = clamp(nframes, 1, q->queued);
 
+	/* a partial chain cannot be unwound: the ring still owns its pages */
+	for (i = 0; i < nframes; i++) {
+		if (!FIELD_GET(NPU_RX_DMA_DESC_DONE_MASK, desc[index].ctrl))
+			return NULL;
+		index = (index + 1) % q->ndesc;
+	}
+	dma_rmb();
+
+	index = q->tail;
 	for (i = 0; i < nframes; i++) {
 		struct mt76_queue_entry *e = &q->entry[index];
 		int len = FIELD_GET(NPU_RX_DMA_DESC_CUR_LEN_MASK,
 				    desc[index].ctrl);
 
-		if (!FIELD_GET(NPU_RX_DMA_DESC_DONE_MASK, desc[index].ctrl)) {
-			dev_kfree_skb(skb);
-			return NULL;
-		}
-
 		dma_sync_single_for_cpu(dev->dma_dev, e->dma_addr[0],
 					e->dma_len[0],
 					page_pool_get_dma_dir(q->page_pool));
